<!--Post: 36--><!--Time: 1085201023-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST36"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Cosmoj<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cosmoj<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, May 21, 2004 - 9:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I posted something similar on the C &amp; J board way back when the March letter first came out, but there are so many more posters now, that maybe someone would to discuss. <BR> <BR>When the letter first came out, Susan Peters &#40;Channel 3 I think&#41; interviewed Drowatsky who is now retired and was the lead detective during the Otero years.   He made an interesting comment that puzzles me and though I can&#39;t recall it verbatim he and Susan were talking about how the investigation was led away from solving the crime as police officers started accusing police officers, etc.  He then said something to the effect that the accusations started because there was a bloody bootprint at the Otero&#39;s which was made by the type of boot that the WPD wore at the time. However, they had concluded that bootprint was there before the officers showed up.  The fact that BTK wore police style boots doesn&#39;t suprise me and really I don&#39;t feel he is a police officer.  What puzzles me is where the blood came from.  I thought they were all strangled and it was reported they each had bruising around the face and cheeks.  I never heard about any of the Otero&#39;s being stabbed or anything else that would cause bleeding.  I would imagine as gruesome as it seems that strangling might cause someone to vomit some blood, I don&#39;t know, but even so, would it be enough blood for there to be a full bootprint?  Any thoughts?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 36-->
<!--Post: 91--><!--Time: 1085540528-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST91"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Clarice<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Clarice<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 25, 2004 - 8:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Cosmoj; I was troubled by the same matter.The kansas.com board has discussed this issue.  The rumor is that there was blood at the Otero house.  It is not resolved if a careless cop stepped in the blood or if BTK had cop boots on.  However, consider the following and draw your own conclusions &#40;read between the lines&#41; based on the assumption that there is probably a lot that LE is not releasing.  BTK had a knife with him as confirmed by the Vian survivors and the Bright survivor.  To me this indicates that he may have carried a knife to the other crimes as well.  Did he use it on the victims?  We know that he did use it on Bright and I think Weigerle &#40;not sure on Weigerle&#41;.   <BR>Binding and strangling would not cause enough blood for a foot print. <BR>On the John Douglas site, he indicates that BTK sent a sketch to the home of the &#34;older woman&#34; that he did not kill &#40;he got tired waiting for her and left- remember this one?&#41;.  The sketch showed the insertion of a Billy Club or &#34;sap&#34; into the victim. <BR>It has been stated that he &#34;spent a lot of time&#34; with Julie Otero.  What if he used a billy club on her and/or Mrs. Otero?  This would cause a female to bleed.   <BR>Sorry for the explicit post, but as I said I had the same question re blood that you had.  This is how I have put it all together. <BR>One more item, John Douglas profiled BTK as a &#34;wanna be cop&#34; for several reasons.  One reason could be the footware?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 91-->
<!--Post: 99--><!--Time: 1085598727-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST99"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Prairie_chicken<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Prairie_chicken<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, May 26, 2004 - 12:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Cosmoj, this is a copy and paste from my post at C&amp;J.  I think it follows some of your ideas:This is bothering me: <BR>        I was looking at the sketches of the Otero house and position of the bodies. The parents and son were found first upstairs. The stairway to the basement started in the hallway and descended down toward the east wall of the basement. The sketches show Josie was hung from pipes close to the west wall of the basement. Now, &#40;I’ve spent all morning trying to find this&#41; from:  <BR> <BR> <BR>The Wichita Eagle--one of the 2004 articles. <BR> <BR>&#34;It was one of the most blood-curdling scenes I ever worked, and I worked a lot of murders in Wichita,&#34; said former Wichita police detective Gary Caldwell, who was among the first on the scene… <BR> <BR>Caldwell didn&#39;t see her when he descended to the basement without a flashlight. Groping around for a light switch, he bumped into the body -- an experience that later gave him nightmares… <BR> <BR>Hoping the killer would return that night: <BR>&#34;It was like Grand Central Station,&#34; said police detective Bernie Drowatzky. He and Caldwell sat in the Otero house all night with a psychic, watching traffic and hoping the killer would return. <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s my put: <BR> <BR>Now, Gary Caldwell was a member of the WPD, one of the first on the scene &#40;was he expecting the call knowing they would be found that afternoon, did he need to be one of the first to explain any possible bloody footprints or fingerprints, did he want to watch it all unfold?&#41; <BR> <BR>Caldwell bumps into Josie’s body in the dark? How could he when at 4:30 or 5:00 in the evening, even in January, there would be some light through the basement windows or from upstairs. It wouldn’t be totally pitch black dark. Besides that, he gets to the bottom of the stairs on the east side of the basement and is still groping for a light switch clear on the other side of the basement? Or maybe he knew she needed to be found over there.  <BR> <BR>Then, he sits in the house all night with a psychic and another detective hoping the killer returns to the scene. The way he loves to play games, that would be the ultimate.  <BR> <BR>Gary Caldwell no longer works for the WPD and I wonder if he is the same Gary Caldwell that is now the Sheriff of Clay Co? <BR> <BR>Would you please check out your 1974 phone directory and see where this guy lived in 1974? <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 99-->
<!--Post: 186--><!--Time: 1086337680-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST186"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->J_eric<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->J_eric<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 04, 2004 - 1:28 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Are you telling us that the police, in investigating a brand-new multiple murder, radioed for a psychic but not a police dog? <BR> <BR>Cops without flashlights?  All Los Angeles officers carry big mag-lights, the better to bop evildoers over the head with.  <BR> <BR>This sounds more like a Moe, Larry and Shemp farce! <BR> <BR> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 186-->
<!--Post: 239--><!--Time: 1086839806-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST239"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Cosmoj<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cosmoj<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, June 09, 2004 - 8:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Clarice,   I have a 1974 city directory which only lists names by addresses and a 1976 phone book.  In the 1976 Phone book there isn&#39;t a listing for a Gary Caldwell, but there is a listing for a GL Caldwell at 1920 e 10th.  I have a 1980 &amp; 1986 city directory and neither lists this person at that address. <BR> <BR>I have often wondered about the light switch also.  There were 5 kids in the family and 3 bedrooms on the main floor.  It&#39;s possible that all 5 shared 2 bedrooms, but I think it&#39;s unlikely.  Which means someone had to have a bedroom in the basement.  Therefore I find it hard to believe there wasn&#39;t a light switch right at the bottom of the stairs.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 239-->
<!--Post: 258--><!--Time: 1086969270-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST258"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jenkins<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jenkins<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 11, 2004 - 8:54 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->well the position of the light switch is impossible to determine at this time without entering that basement. <BR> <BR>Knowing the area, I would guess that the basement was unfinished and knowing how unfinished basements are around here its not out of the question for me to guess that the light was just a bulb protruding from the ceiling and that it had a string or cord of sorts to be used for turning it on or off, in which case you would have to walk around a bit to find. I mean you would expect there to be a light switch at the top or bottom of the stairs, but that is a bit of a shabby area and its not out of the question that the basement was an unfinished one with merely a cord for turning on the light. As far as the theory on how dark it was in the basement, there is really no way to tell. The living room in my parents basement is pitch black dark as there is not a single window. There are 3 windows in that basement but they are in other rooms. Now I do believe that the basement of the Otero house was probably just one big room and that the windows should have provided a bit of light at 4:30 or 5. Not much but enough to see a body. However, in a shoddy neighborhood, you generally have shoddy insulation. It is not uncommon for people to put things in their basement windows to prevent air from leaking in or out. In the basement of my parents house, each of those windows is entirely blocked by thick styrofoam, thus making the basement pitch black anytime of day anytime of the year. It is not unlikely that the Oteros did the same. <BR> <BR>Oh and J, yes it is very much a 3 stooges operation here, but god bless&#39;em they sure do try and hell they even worked without a contract for a bit here recently, good boys in blue good boys, just not the best and the brightest. <BR> <BR> <BR>Still lacking coherency sorry guys. Maybe after lunch I&#39;ll make more sense.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 258-->
<!--Post: 982--><!--Time: 1094218277-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST982"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, September 03, 2004 - 6:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Clarice: I read last night on another message board that he did bad things to Julie Otero over and above what we know. The person stating that said that someone involved in the investigation told her &#39;more than she wanted to know&#39;. I will try to locate the site again. Evidently, in one of BTK&#39;s letters he talked about Julie and how she excited him with her &#39;pleading eyes&#39; as he tightened the rope around her neck. <BR> <BR>Should the police now release publicly what has been known about the case, the outcry would be horrendous. Again and again, the tax payers are duped by public officials and those who are sworn to protect and serve. There is prudence in avoiding a panic, but forewarned is forearmed.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 982-->
